WEBVTT

00:00:00.752 --> 00:00:05.025
llama.cpp is a lightweight local inference project for running large language models on

00:00:05.025 --> 00:00:05.980
personal computers,

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.810
workstations, servers, and homelabs.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:12.155
It is popular because it makes local model execution practical without requiring a large

00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:13.050
managed platform.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.470
A user can load a compatible model file, send prompts to the runtime,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.380
and experiment with language model behavior on hardware they control.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.630
Local inference is different from using a hosted AI API.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.505
With a hosted service, the provider operates the model, runtime, scaling layer,

00:00:26.505 --> 00:00:28.145
and much of the infrastructure.

00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:32.090
With llama.cpp, the user controls the model file, runtime version, hardware,

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.905
performance settings, and deployment pattern.

00:00:33.905 --> 00:00:36.815
That control can improve privacy, support offline workflows,

00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:40.829
reduce dependency on external services, and make experimentation more transparent.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:45.330
llama.cpp is closely associated with GGUF model files and local model serving.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.748
GGUF packages model weights and metadata in a format that compatible runtimes can load.

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:53.715
llama.cpp can run models interactively, expose local server endpoints,

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:56.625
and act as a foundation for web UIs or applications.

00:00:56.625 --> 00:01:00.465
The file, runtime, prompt formatting, and hardware all have to fit together.

00:01:00.465 --> 00:01:02.535
It helps to separate three levels of use.

00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:05.490
Running a model means loading it and getting output from a prompt.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.800
Serving a model means making that inference available through an API or local service.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.880
Building an application means wrapping that service with user workflows, data handling,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.195
access control, logging, and reliability expectations.

00:01:17.195 --> 00:01:21.410
Getting started with llama.cpp is about learning all three levels without losing sight of

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:22.700
repeatability and safety.
